    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_webf_311",
      "name": "contrast-rules-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Pass or fail? Reading WCAG contrast results",
      "description": "Two decisions from the brand-refresh audit: whether a ratio that displays as almost 4.5:1 passes, and which parts of a form must meet a contrast requirement at all.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The new helper text under the email field is 14px, regular weight, colour #777777 on a #ffffff background. The DevTools colour picker shows a contrast ratio of 4.48. The designer argues: \"That rounds to 4.5, so it meets AA.\" What is the correct verdict for 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)?",
          "explanation": "WCAG thresholds are minimums and the ratio is not rounded before comparing: 4.48:1 is below 4.5:1, so the text fails. 14px regular is normal-size text (large text starts at 24px regular or about 18.67px bold), so the 3:1 large-text threshold does not apply, and helper text is not exempt: it is real content that people need to read. The smallest change is a slightly darker grey: #767676 on white is about 4.54:1 and passes.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "It fails: 4.48:1 is below the 4.5:1 minimum, and ratios are not rounded up before the comparison.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "It passes: WCAG ratios are compared after rounding to one decimal place.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "It passes: helper text only needs 3:1 because it is secondary information.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "It fails only AAA (7:1); AA needs 4.5:1 for large text and 3:1 for normal text.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The checkout form has these parts. Under WCAG 2.2 level AA, which of them MUST meet a contrast requirement (1.4.3 for text, 1.4.11 for non-text)? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "Placeholder text is text, and 1.4.3 has no exception for it, so it needs 4.5:1 (better still, do not use it for anything a user must read). When a 1px border is the only thing that shows where a text input is, it is visual information needed to identify the component, so 1.4.11 asks for 3:1 against the adjacent colour. A focus indicator is visual information needed to identify a state, so it is covered by 1.4.11 too. Text in an inactive (disabled) control is explicitly exempt from 1.4.3, and so are logotypes - the text inside the shop's logo has no contrast requirement.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "The grey placeholder text \"name@example.com\" inside the email field",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "The 1px border that is the only visual sign of where the email field is",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "The outline drawn around the Checkout button when it has keyboard focus",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "The label of the \"Place order\" button while it is disabled",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The shop's name inside its logo in the header",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
